The rapid advancement of machine learning has led to the development of numerous algorithms designed to detect anomalies in streaming data. These algorithms are typically categorized into three types: supervised, semi-supervised, and unsupervised. Supervised methods require labeled training data, whereas semi-supervised and unsupervised approaches operate without such labels. While semi-supervised and unsupervised methods are generally preferred for real-world applications due to the scarcity of labeled data, this study focuses on semi-supervised techniques.

Given the growing demand for real-time anomaly detection, there is a need to assess the performance of these algorithms across various metrics, including false positive rates, false detection rates, and computational efficiency. This work aims to provide a comprehensive evaluation of the most common algorithms for real-time anomaly detection in streaming IoT sensor data.

The paper is structured as follows: Section \ref{sec:background} offers a background overview of anomaly detection algorithms. Section \ref{sec:dataset} details the dataset used for evaluation. Section \ref{sec:experimental} presents the experimental setup and results. Finally, Section \ref{sec:conclusion} provides a conclusion and outlines future work.

\section{Background}
Anomaly detection involves identifying data points that deviate from a defined pattern. These patterns can be derived from a single dataset or by combining multiple datasets. Anomalies are generally classified into two categories: point anomalies, which are individual data points that deviate from the pattern, and contextual anomalies, which are normal in general but anomalous within a specific context.

